  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Evidence for each step of the chronic‑HBV → inflammation → fibrosis/cirrhosis → mutation‑accumulation → HCC pathway**\n\n- **Chronic HBV infection drives persistent hepatic inflammation.**  \n  Human studies describe “recurrent liver inflammation caused by host immune responses during chronic HBV infection” and “immune reactions that lead to repeated hepatic inflammation”【PMID: 29052129】. Review of HBV‑related carcinogenesis repeatedly cites chronic inflammation as a primary consequence of long‑lasting infection【PMID: 20485626】, and transcriptomic analysis of CHB patients shows up‑regulation of inflammation‑associated hypoxia genes that are absent in acute infection models, implicating inflammatory mediators in the chronic state【PMID: 32839523】.\n\n- **Chronic inflammation promotes fibrosis and cirrhosis.**  \n  The same reviews note that sustained inflammation “leads to liver fibrosis and cirrhosis”【PMID: 29052129】【PMID: 28097530】, and that inflammatory cytokines are central to the development of fibrosis and hepatic regeneration【PMID: 20485626】. Animal models of HBV‑transgenic mice develop liver tumors after prolonged inflammatory signaling, supporting a causal link between inflammation‑driven fibrogenesis and oncogenesis【PMID: 39290403】 (although this model also shows inflammation‑independent tumorigenesis, it confirms that inflammation can be sufficient).\n\n- **Fibrosis/cirrhosis accelerates hepatocyte turnover and mutation accumulation.**  \n  Chronic inflammation “accelerates hepatocyte turnover rate and promotes accumulation of mutations”【PMID: 29052129】. A mechanistic review states that “hepatic regeneration induced by cellular immune responses may favour the accumulation of genetic alterations” in infected hepatocytes【PMID: 16461228】. These processes provide the substrate for oncogenic mutations.\n\n- **Mutation‑bearing hepatocytes evolve into HCC.**  \n  Epidemiological and mechanistic data link the combined effect of “chronic liver inflammation, fibrosis and regeneration” to the emergence of HCC in HBV carriers【PMID: 30611768】. The high genomic instability and frequent insertional mutagenesis observed in HBV‑related tumors are attributed to the mutational burden generated during chronic inflammatory regeneration【"
  },
